 "text": "Data contacts Rachele.Poggi@itf-oecd.org tel: +33 (0)1 73 31 25 54\n\nITF 2 rue André Pascal, 75775 Paris Cedex 16, France www.itf-oecd.org\n\nFreight Transport in 2025 In 2025, the global economy continued its steady recovery despite heterogeneity between regions. Global real GDP grew by 3.4% between 2024 and 2025, 1.9% in advanced economies and 4.4% in emerging and developing economies.\n\nWorld trade volumes increased by 4.6%, and air freight tonne-kilometres (T-km) by 3.4% in 2025 compared to 2024, a less substantial growth than in 2024. The increase is driven largely by the strong performance of Asia Pacific. Regarding surface transport, the EU27 continued to register a decrease compared to last year’s transport. Rail transport, measured in tonne-kilometres, decreased by 2.3% in 2025 compared to 2024, and road transport by 0.3%.\n\n## Key Transport Statistics\n\n### 2025 Data\n\nNumber of Road Fatalities in 2025 (percentage change from previous year)\n\nRoad Safety Measures Effective in Reducing Fatalities in 2025 National speed limits, authorised blood alcohol content, seat-belt and helmet laws, and infrastructure improvements have been key drivers, among other policies, in reducing road fatalities during the last decades. A comparison of road deaths between 2025 and 2024 shows an improvement in road safety in 22 countries out of 39 with available data. The most significant reduction in road deaths was recorded in Estonia (-36%), Greece (-22%), and Switzerland (-14%).\n\nAir and Sea Freight Volumes in 2025 EU27 freight transport showed a mixed performance in 2025. In December 2025, air freight closed 2025 26% higher than February 2020 levels. On the other hand, maritime transport in the EU27 has mostly remained below the benchmark due to international turmoil and sluggish economic growth in the years following the pandemic. Maritime freight transport in December 2025 registered levels 10% lower than the pre-pandemic benchmark. US freight transport has shown signs of resilience in the post-pandemic period, staying above the levels registered in February 2020 thanks to a rapid economic recovery. In December 2025, air freight was 17% higher than in February 2020. Maritime transport has been steadily increasing for the last 4 years, reaching volumes 9% bigger than the benchmark.\n\n2026\n\n-40 -30 -20 -10 0 10 20 30 40 50\n\nEstonia \nGreece \nSwitzerland \nPoland \nRomania \nNew Zealand \nAzerbaijan \nMoldova \nHungary \nNorth Macedonia \nUnited States \nDenmark \nPortugal \nBulgaria \nItaly \nJapan \nSerbia, Republic of \nCzech Republic \nSweden \nBelgium \nIceland \nLuxembourg \nGermany \nAustralia \nFrance \nFinland \nIsrael \nLatvia \nGeorgia \nIreland \nCroatia \nNetherlands \nLithuania \nAustria \nAlbania \nNorway \nBosnia-Herzegovina \nSlovenia \nMalta \n\n-40 -30 -20 -10 0 10 20 30 40 50\n\nSource: International Transport Forum\n\nGDP, Trade and Freight Transport in 2025 (Percentage change from 2024 to 2025)\n\nExternal Trade by Sea and Air (percentage change from February 2020, kilograms, monthly trend, seasonally adjusted)\n\nSource: IMF (GDP), WTO (trade volume), IATA (air T-km), ITF (rail and road tkm).\n\nNote: rail T-km do not include BEL, CYP, MLT, POL. Road T-km do not include CYP, POL, and 3 quarters for AUT, BEL, DNL, EST, DEU, GRC, IRL, ITA, NLD, ROU.\n\nSource: Eurostat and the U.S. Census Bureau (Data processed by the International Transport Forum).\n\nWorld EU\n\nTrade Air T-km volume\n\nGDP GDP Rail T-km Road T-km\n\n3.4%\n\n1.4%\n\n-2.3% -0.3%\n\n4.6%\n\n3.4%\n\nUS Air US Sea EU Air EU Sea\n\nJan 05\n\n0%\n\n20%\n\nDec 25 +9% -10% +17% +26%\n\nKey Transport Statistics • 2025 Data International Transport Forum\n\n| COUNTRIES | RAIL | | ROAD | | INLAND WATERWAYS |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| | | Goods Transport (million T-km) | Passengers Carried | Goods Transport (million T-km) Traffic (million First Registration (number) | Fuel Deliveries (1000 Tons) Road Accident Goods Transp